<p>Margaret Bowers was born May 20, 1865, in Whitewater, Wisconsin, to John R. and Sarah Bowers. Her family moved to Monona, Iowa, where she attended grade school. She began teaching at age 17 and taught in Iowa for two years before moving to Mandan in time for the 1884–85 school year.</p>

<figure class="article-figure article-figure-left">
  <img src="/assets/images/margaret-bowers-bingenheimer.jpg" alt="Printed biographical account of Margaret Bowers Bingenheimer">
</figure>

<p>Bowers was assigned to the West End school at Main Street and Eighth Avenue Northwest. The two-room, two-story school had been converted from a house. As primary teacher and principal, she taught about 25 first- and second-grade pupils on the ground floor and earned $55 per month. Ida Ellison taught grades three through five.</p>

<p>Students above fifth grade attended the East End school, a four-room frame building at First Avenue Northwest and Second Street that later became City Hall. Mr. Parsons was principal and supervised three other teachers. Both early school buildings later burned.</p>

<p>Margaret married {% person "george-h-bingenheimer" %}George H. Bingenheimer{% endperson %}, then Morton County treasurer, on September 16, 1885.</p>

<p>Margaret Bingenheimer served on the library board from its organization and on the Mandan Hospital board until the Methodist Church assumed operation of the hospital. She was also a trustee of the First Presbyterian Church.</p>

<p>Her parents and brothers, John Jr. and Gerald Bowers, moved to Mandan around 1890. Her father, a cabinetmaker, had retired by then. Gerald lived in Mandan until 1905, spent 30 years in Minneapolis, and returned in 1935. John Jr. made his home in Mandan beginning in 1890 and worked in the abstract business.</p>
